bP7DuhzsrYbJui0begxAlNhZKvT82gqAOc5X5we0lZhaGXQnaKh6YNt_Y9CDauPVPG3V9oFsFtjJS1uJ9HMdM9zfyoXtW8jpMvZPh0LZCJ3hkSHPFav6dOBxOJ45DgEUJSshFIN1V0N0cFlRc4pKEdDLHtv3hYQMnUCPRr-0u"},{"type":"message","id":"msg_0260ceffca0ed12f016adef872e46481919223dc1d59a723ae","role":"assistant","status":"completed","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"I have read the full supplied proof and the NL0 authoring rules. I am preserving its five-phase route, including the localization argument for an arbitrary admissible lattice, the rank-one calculation, and the characteristic-2 endpoint. I have also checked pinned mathlib sources for the principal basis, dimension, scalar-extension, and factorial facts used below.","annotations":[],"logprobs":[]}],"phase":"commentary"},{"type":"function_call","id":"fc_0260ceffca0ed12f016adef8754a44819180dc78126d1751a9","call_id":"call_2f141e87e23d42c5b889a3c94862920a","name":"check_draft","arguments":"{\"artifact\":{\"spec\":{\"format_version\":2,\"case_id\":\"lie-module-irreducibility\",\"full_target\":\"For every prime p, every field K of characteristic p, every integer λ with 0≤λ<p, the complex irreducible highest-weight sl₂(C)-module V=V(λ) of dimension λ+1 with Chevalley generators x,y,h, every nonzero v∈V with xv=0 and hv=λv, and every arbitrary admissible lattice M⊂V (a free abelian subgroup with an integer basis that is also a complex basis, stable under the integer enveloping subalgebra generated by all x^r/r!,y^r/r!), prove that V(K)=M⊗ℤK is nonzero and every K-linear subspace invariant under x,y,h is zero or V(K). Here L(K)=(ℤx+ℤy+ℤh)⊗ℤK acts by the induced lattice operators, including the Chevalley construction in characteristic 2. No algebraic closure assumption on K is made, and λ=0 and p=2 are included.\",\"conclusion\":\"V(K) is nonzero, and for every K-linear subspace W⊂V(K), if W is invariant under the induced operators x,y,h, then W={0} or W=V(K). Thus V(K) is an irreducible L(K)-module, including λ=0 and p=2, over the given arbitrary field K.\",\"parameters\":[{\"symbol\":\"p\",\"type\":\"natural number\"},{\"symbol\":\"K\",\"type\":\"field\"},{\"symbol\":\"λ\",\"type\":\"integer\"},{\"symbol\":\"V\",\"type\":\"complex vector space with a representation of sl₂(C)\"},{\"symbol\":\"x\",\"type\":\"element of sl₂(C), acting on V\"},{\"symbol\":\"y\",\"type\":\"element of sl₂(C), acting on V\"},{\"symbol\":\"h\",\"type\":\"element of sl₂(C), acting on V\"},{\"symbol\":\"v\",\"type\":\"vector in V\"},{\"symbol\":\"M\",\"type\":\"additive subgroup of V\"}],\"hypotheses\":[{\"id\":\"H001\",\"statement\":\"p is prime.\",\"origin\":\"problem.md:16\"},{\"id\":\"H002\",\"statement\":\"The characteristic of the field K is p.\",\"origin\":\"problem.md:16\"},{\"id\":\"H003\",\"statement\":\"0≤λ<p.\",\"origin\":\"problem.md:17\"},{\"id\":\"H004\",\"statement\":\"V=V(λ) is the complex irreducible highest-weight sl₂(C)-module of highest weight λ, of complex dimension λ+1. The elements x,y,h are its Chevalley generators and satisfy [h,x]=2x, [h,y]=-2y, [x,y]=h.\",\"origin\":\"problem.md:18-24\"},{\"id\":\"H005\",\"statement\":\"v≠0, xv=0, and hv=λv.\",\"origin\":\"problem.md:26-27\"},{\"id\":\"H006\",\"statement\":\"M is an admissible lattice: it is a free abelian subgroup of V with an integer basis that is also a complex basis of V, and is stable under U_Z. It is arbitrary and is not required to be the minimal lattice.\",\"origin\":\"problem.md:30-32\"}],\"definitions\":[{\"id\":\"D001\",\"symbol\":\"n\",\"type\":\"integer\",\"definition\":\"n=λ+1.\",\"depends_on\":[],\"requires\":[],\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[\"λ\"]},{\"id\":\"D002\",\"symbol\":\"I\",\"type\":\"finite set of integers\",\"definition\":\"I={i∈ℤ:0≤i≤λ}. Factorials and binomial coefficients with indices in this range are those of the corresponding nonnegative integers.\",\"depends_on\":[],\"requires\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"i\",\"type\":\"integer\"}],\"objects\":[\"λ\",\"i\"]},{\"id\":\"D003\",\"symbol\":\"X\",\"type\":\"family of complex linear endomorphisms of V indexed by nonnegative integers\",\"definition\":\"For each integer r≥0, X_r=x^r/r!, where powers denote composition of the action of x and X_0=Id_V.\",\"depends_on\":[],\"requires\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"r\",\"type\":\"nonnegative integer\"}],\"objects\":[\"r\",\"x\",\"V\"]},{\"id\":\"D004\",\"symbol\":\"Y\",\"type\":\"family of complex linear endomorphisms of V indexed by nonnegative integers\",\"definition\":\"For each integer r≥0, Y_r=y^r/r!, where powers denote composition of the action of y and Y_0=Id_V.\",\"depends_on\":[],\"requires\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"r\",\"type\":\"nonnegative integer\"}],\"objects\":[\"r\",\"y\",\"V\"]},{\"id\":\"D005\",\"symbol\":\"U_Z\",\"type\":\"integer subalgebra of the universal enveloping algebra of sl₂(C), with its action on V\",\"definition\":\"U_Z is the ℤ-subalgebra of the universal enveloping algebra generated by all divided powers x^r/r!,y^r/r! for integers r≥0; their actions on V are X_r,Y_r.\",\"depends_on\":[\"D003\",\"D004\"],\"requires\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"r\",\"type\":\"nonnegative integer\"}],\"objects\":[\"r\",\"x\",\"y\",\"V\",\"X\",\"Y\"]},{\"id\":\"D006\",\"symbol\":\"e\",\"type\":\"family I→V\",\"definition\":\"For i∈I, e_i=y^iv/i!=Y_i v.\",\"depends_on\":[\"D002\",\"D004\"],\"requires\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"i\",\"type\":\"element of I\"}],\"objects\":[\"i\",\"I\",\"y\",\"v\",\"Y\"]},{\"id\":\"D007\",\"symbol\":\"R\",\"type\":\"subring of ℚ, also embedded in ℂ\",\"definition\":\"R=ℤ_(p)={a/b∈ℚ:a,b∈ℤ, b≠0, p does not divide b}, with the usual rational arithmetic and its embedding into ℂ.\",\"depends_on\":[],\"requires\":[\"H001\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"a\",\"type\":\"integer\"},{\"symbol\":\"b\",\"type\":\"nonzero integer\"}],\"objects\":[\"p\",\"a\",\"b\"]},{\"id\":\"D008\",\"symbol\":\"M_R\",\"type\":\"R-module\",\"definition\":\"M_R=R⊗_ℤ M. Once its multiplication map into V is proved injective, it is identified with its image in V.\",\"depends_on\":[\"D007\"],\"requires\":[\"H001\"],\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[\"R\",\"M\",\"V\"]},{\"id\":\"D009\",\"symbol\":\"ι_R\",\"type\":\"R-linear map M_R→V\",\"definition\":\"ι_R is the tensor multiplication map determined by ι_R(r⊗m)=r m for r∈R and m∈M, using the rational scalar action on V.\",\"depends_on\":[\"D007\",\"D008\"],\"requires\":[\"H001\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"r\",\"type\":\"element of R\"},{\"symbol\":\"m\",\"type\":\"element of M\"}],\"objects\":[\"r\",\"m\",\"R\",\"M\",\"M_R\",\"V\"]},{\"id\":\"D010\",\"symbol\":\"E\",\"type\":\"family of complex linear endomorphisms of V indexed by I×I\",\"definition\":\"For i,j∈I, E_ij is the unique complex linear map sending e_j to e_i and every e_k with k∈I and k≠j to zero. Its existence and uniqueness use the complex basis asserted by N001.\",\"depends_on\":[\"D002\",\"D006\"],\"requires\":[\"N001\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"i\",\"type\":\"element of I\"},{\"symbol\":\"j\",\"type\":\"element of I\"},{\"symbol\":\"k\",\"type\":\"element of I\"}],\"objects\":[\"i\",\"j\",\"k\",\"I\",\"e\",\"V\"]},{\"id\":\"D011\",\"symbol\":\"Jcoord\",\"type\":\"family of R-submodules of ℂ indexed by I\",\"definition\":\"For i∈I, Jcoord_i={c∈ℂ:c e_i∈ι_R(M_R)}.\",\"depends_on\":[\"D002\",\"D006\",\"D007\",\"D008\",\"D009\"],\"requires\":[\"H001\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"i\",\"type\":\"element of I\"},{\"symbol\":\"c\",\"type\":\"complex number\"}],\"objects\":[\"i\",\"c\",\"I\",\"e\",\"ι_R\",\"M_R\",\"R\"]},{\"id\":\"D012\",\"symbol\":\"J\",\"type\":\"R-submodule of ℂ\",\"definition\":\"J={c∈ℂ:c v∈ι_R(M_R)}. Since e_0=v when 0∈I, this is precisely Jcoord_0 in that case; the equality of all coordinate modules will be proved, not assumed.\",\"depends_on\":[\"D007\",\"D008\",\"D009\"],\"requires\":[\"H001\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"c\",\"type\":\"complex number\"}],\"objects\":[\"c\",\"v\",\"ι_R\",\"M_R\",\"R\"]},{\"id\":\"D013\",\"symbol\":\"V(K)\",\"type\":\"K-vector space with induced lattice operators\",\"definition\":\"V(K)=M⊗_ℤ K. An operator on M preserving M acts by its tensor extension on V(K); the same letters x,y,h denote these induced actions when their lattice preservation has been established.\",\"depends_on\":[],\"requires\":[],\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[\"M\",\"K\",\"x\",\"y\",\"h\"]},{\"id\":\"D014\",\"symbol\":\"L(K)\",\"type\":\"Lie algebra over K\",\"definition\":\"L(K)=(ℤx+ℤy+ℤh)⊗_ℤ K, the scalar extension of the Chevalley ℤ-form with the commutator bracket inherited from sl₂(C). This is the Chevalley sl₂ construction also when the characteristic is 2. Its action on V(K) is induced from the actions on M.\",\"depends_on\":[\"D013\"],\"requires\":[],\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[\"x\",\"y\",\"h\",\"K\",\"M\",\"V(K)\"]}],\"logic\":{\"classical\":true,\"rules\":[\"direct\",\"forall_introduction\",\"implication_introduction\",\"exists_elimination\",\"case_elimination\",\"natural_induction\"]},\"pins\":{},\"inputs\":[]},\"graph\":{\"format_version\":2,\"case_id\":\"lie-module-irreducibility\",\"spec_sha256\":\"controller-bound\",\"propositions\":[{\"id\":\"H001\",\"kind\":\"hypothesis\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[\"p\"]},{\"id\":\"H002\",\"kind\":\"hypothesis\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[\"K\",\"p\"]},{\"id\":\"H003\",\"kind\":\"hypothesis\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[\"λ\",\"p\"]},{\"id\":\"H004\",\"kind\":\"hypothesis\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[\"V\",\"λ\",\"x\",\"y\",\"h\"]},{\"id\":\"H005\",\"kind\":\"hypothesis\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[\"v\",\"x\",\"h\",\"λ\"]},{\"id\":\"H006\",\"kind\":\"hypothesis\",\"definitions\":[\"D005\"],\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[\"M\",\"V\",\"U_Z\"]},{\"id\":\"M001\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"Let A be a commutative semiring, B a semiring with an A-algebra structure, T an additive commutative monoid with an A-module structure, and f an A-basis of T indexed by a type Q. The canonical base-changed basis f.baseChange B is a B-basis of B⊗_A T, and for each q∈Q its q-th vector is 1⊗f_q.\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"A\",\"type\":\"commutative semiring\"},{\"symbol\":\"B\",\"type\":\"semiring and A-algebra\"},{\"symbol\":\"T\",\"type\":\"additive commutative monoid and A-module\"},{\"symbol\":\"Q\",\"type\":\"index type\"},{\"symbol\":\"f\",\"type\":\"A-basis of T indexed by Q\"},{\"symbol\":\"q\",\"type\":\"element of Q\"}],\"objects\":[\"A\",\"B\",\"T\",\"Q\",\"f\",\"q\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"Module.Basis.baseChange_apply\",\"hypotheses\":[\"A is a commutative semiring; B is a semiring with an A-algebra structure.\",\"T is an additive commutative monoid with an A-module structure.\",\"f is an A-basis of T indexed by Q; q is in Q.\"]}},{\"id\":\"M002\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"For a semiring A satisfying the strong rank condition, an additive commutative monoid T with an A-module structure, a finite index type Q and an A-basis f of T indexed by Q, Module.finrank_A(T)=card(Q).\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"A\",\"type\":\"semiring satisfying the strong rank condition\"},{\"symbol\":\"T\",\"type\":\"additive commutative monoid and A-module\"},{\"symbol\":\"Q\",\"type\":\"finite index type\"},{\"symbol\":\"f\",\"type\":\"A-basis of T indexed by Q\"}],\"objects\":[\"A\",\"T\",\"Q\",\"f\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"Module.finrank_eq_card_basis\",\"hypotheses\":[\"A is a semiring satisfying the strong rank condition.\",\"T is an additive commutative monoid with an A-module structure.\",\"Q is a finite index type and f is an A-basis of T indexed by Q.\"]}},{\"id\":\"M003\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"Let A be a division ring, T an additive commutative group and A-module, Q a finite nonempty type, and f:Q→T a linearly independent family over A. If card(Q)=Module.finrank_A(T), then the A-linear span of range(f) is all of T.\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"A\",\"type\":\"division ring\"},{\"symbol\":\"T\",\"type\":\"additive commutative group and A-module\"},{\"symbol\":\"Q\",\"type\":\"finite nonempty type\"},{\"symbol\":\"f\",\"type\":\"family Q→T\"}],\"objects\":[\"A\",\"T\",\"Q\",\"f\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"LinearIndependent.span_eq_top_of_card_eq_finrank\",\"hypotheses\":[\"A is a division ring and T is an additive commutative group with an A-module structure.\",\"Q is finite and nonempty.\",\"f is linearly independent over A.\",\"card(Q)=Module.finrank_A(T).\"]}},{\"id\":\"M004\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"Let A be a semiring satisfying the strong rank condition, Q a finite type, and T_q for q∈Q a family of additive commutative monoids with A-module structures. If each T_q is free and finitely generated over A, then Module.finrank_A(∏_{q∈Q}T_q)=∑_{q∈Q}Module.finrank_A(T_q).\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"A\",\"type\":\"semiring satisfying the strong rank condition\"},{\"symbol\":\"Q\",\"type\":\"finite index type\"},{\"symbol\":\"T\",\"type\":\"family of additive commutative monoids and A-modules indexed by Q\"},{\"symbol\":\"q\",\"type\":\"element of Q\"}],\"objects\":[\"A\",\"Q\",\"T\",\"q\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"Module.finrank_pi_fintype\",\"hypotheses\":[\"A is a semiring satisfying the strong rank condition.\",\"Q is finite and each T_q is an additive commutative monoid with an A-module structure.\",\"Each T_q is free over A.\",\"Each T_q is finitely generated over A.\"]}},{\"id\":\"M005\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"For any semiring A, additive commutative monoids T and U with A-module structures, and A-linear equivalence f:T≃U, Module.finrank_A(T)=Module.finrank_A(U).\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"A\",\"type\":\"semiring\"},{\"symbol\":\"T\",\"type\":\"additive commutative monoid and A-module\"},{\"symbol\":\"U\",\"type\":\"additive commutative monoid and A-module\"},{\"symbol\":\"f\",\"type\":\"A-linear equivalence T≃U\"}],\"objects\":[\"A\",\"T\",\"U\",\"f\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"LinearEquiv.finrank_eq\",\"hypotheses\":[\"A is a semiring; T and U are additive commutative monoids with A-module structures.\",\"f is an A-linear equivalence.\"]}},{\"id\":\"M006\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"For natural numbers ℓ and t, if ℓ is prime and t<ℓ, then ℓ is coprime to t!.\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"ℓ\",\"type\":\"natural number\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"natural number\"}],\"objects\":[\"ℓ\",\"t\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"Nat.Prime.coprime_factorial_of_lt\",\"hypotheses\":[\"ℓ is prime.\",\"t<ℓ.\"]}},{\"id\":\"M007\",\"kind\":\"mathlib\",\"statement\":\"For every division ring A and every additive commutative group T with an A-module structure, T is a free A-module.\",\"definitions\":[],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"A\",\"type\":\"division ring\"},{\"symbol\":\"T\",\"type\":\"additive commutative group and A-module\"}],\"objects\":[\"A\",\"T\"],\"source\":{\"declaration\":\"Module.Free.of_divisionRing\",\"hypotheses\":[\"A is a division ring.\",\"T is an additive commutative group with an A-module structure.\"]}},{\"id\":\"N001\",\"kind\":\"internal\",\"statement\":\"n=λ+1>0 and card(I)=n. The vectors (e_i)_{i∈I} form a complex basis of V; e_0=v. For every integer m≥0, h y^m v=(λ-2m)y^m v; for m≥1, x y^m v=m(λ-m+1)y^{m-1}v. The vectors y^m v vanish for m>λ. The action matrices are xe_0=0, xe_i=(λ-i+1)e_{i-1} for 1≤i≤λ, ye_i=(i+1)e_{i+1} for 0≤i<λ, ye_λ=0, and he_i=(λ-2i)e_i. For all r≥0 and i∈I, X_r e_i=binom(λ-i+r,r)e_{i-r} if r≤i and is zero otherwise; Y_r e_i=binom(i+r,r)e_{i+r} if i+r≤λ and is zero otherwise. All these divided-power matrix coefficients are integers.\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D002\",\"D003\",\"D004\",\"D006\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"m\",\"type\":\"nonnegative integer\"},{\"symbol\":\"i\",\"type\":\"element of I\"},{\"symbol\":\"r\",\"type\":\"nonnegative integer\"}],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"λ\",\"I\",\"e\",\"v\",\"V\",\"x\",\"y\",\"h\",\"X\",\"Y\",\"m\",\"i\",\"r\"],\"origin\":\"problem.md:45-84; explicit commutator induction and basis argument.\"},{\"id\":\"N002\",\"kind\":\"internal\",\"statement\":\"ι_R:M_R→V is injective, its image spans V over ℂ, and M_R is free of rank n over R. Under this embedding M_R is stable under all X_r,Y_r (r≥0). For every integer t with 0≤t≤λ, t! is a unit in R. For all i,j∈I, binom(λ,j) is a unit in R, Y_λ X_λ=E_{λλ}, X_{λ-i}E_{λλ}=E_{iλ}, E_{λλ}Y_{λ-j}=binom(λ,j)E_{λj}, and E_ij=binom(λ,j)^{-1}X_{λ-i}Y_λ X_λ Y_{λ-j}. In particular every E_ij preserves M_R. The operators x,y,h preserve M and give its induced Chevalley action after tensoring.\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D002\",\"D003\",\"D004\",\"D007\",\"D008\",\"D009\",\"D010\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"r\",\"type\":\"nonnegative integer\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"integer with 0≤t≤λ\"},{\"symbol\":\"i\",\"type\":\"element of I\"},{\"symbol\":\"j\",\"type\":\"element of I\"}],\"objects\":[\"ι_R\",\"M_R\",\"V\",\"M\",\"n\",\"R\",\"X\",\"Y\",\"λ\",\"I\",\"E\",\"x\",\"y\",\"h\",\"r\",\"t\",\"i\",\"j\"],\"origin\":\"problem.md:86-116; basis and tensor identification made explicit.\"},{\"id\":\"N003\",\"kind\":\"internal\",\"statement\":\"n>0. All the R-submodules Jcoord_i (i∈I) equal J. J is nonzero and finitely generated over R. Inside V one has M_R=⊕_{i∈I}J e_i: explicitly every element of M_R has a unique expression ∑_{i∈I}c_i e_i with c_i∈J, and every such finite sum belongs to M_R. Put T=ℚ⊗_R J. The multiplication map T→ℂ, q⊗c↦qc, is injective, T is finite dimensional over ℚ and dim_ℚ T=1. For every nonzero a∈J and every c∈J there exists q∈ℚ with c=qa.\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D002\",\"D006\",\"D007\",\"D008\",\"D009\",\"D011\",\"D012\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"i\",\"type\":\"element of I\"},{\"symbol\":\"c\",\"type\":\"element of J\"},{\"symbol\":\"a\",\"type\":\"nonzero element of J\"},{\"symbol\":\"q\",\"type\":\"rational number\"},{\"symbol\":\"T\",\"type\":\"ℚ-vector space ℚ⊗_R J\"}],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"I\",\"Jcoord\",\"J\",\"M_R\",\"e\",\"V\",\"R\",\"T\",\"i\",\"c\",\"a\",\"q\"],\"origin\":\"problem.md:118-140, including the essential rank-one argument rather than inferring rank from containment in ℂ.\"},{\"id\":\"N004\",\"kind\":\"internal\",\"statement\":\"There exists b∈J with b≠0 such that J=Rb. For any such b, the vectors (b e_i)_{i∈I} form an R-basis of M_R (identified with its image under ι_R), of cardinality n>0.\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D002\",\"D006\",\"D007\",\"D008\",\"D009\",\"D012\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"b\",\"type\":\"element of J\"},{\"symbol\":\"i\",\"type\":\"element of I\"}],\"objects\":[\"J\",\"R\",\"b\",\"e\",\"I\",\"M_R\",\"ι_R\",\"n\",\"i\"],\"origin\":\"problem.md:142-148; finite valuation-minimum proof.\"},{\"id\":\"N005\",\"kind\":\"internal\",\"statement\":\"The integer map ℤ→K extends to ρ:R→K, ρ(a/d)=bar(a)/bar(d). There are natural K-linear isomorphisms V(K)=M⊗_ℤK ≃ M_R⊗_R K ≃ (M_R/pM_R)⊗_{ℱ_p}K, where ℱ_p=ℤ/pℤ acts on K by the prime-field embedding. V(K) has a K-basis β=(β_i)_{i∈I} of cardinality n>0, obtained from b e_i for a nonzero b∈J with J=Rb, and hence dim_K V(K)=n. On β the actions x,y,h and all divided powers X_r,Y_r have exactly the integer matrices of N001 reduced to K: xβ_0=0, xβ_i=bar(λ-i+1)β_{i-1} for 1≤i≤λ; yβ_i=bar(i+1)β_{i+1} for i<λ, yβ_λ=0, hβ_i=bar(λ-2i)β_i; X_rβ_i=bar(binom(λ-i+r,r))β_{i-r} for r≤i and zero otherwise, Y_rβ_i=bar(binom(i+r,r))β_{i+r} for i+r≤λ and zero otherwise. For 0≤r≤λ, bar(r!)≠0, X_r=bar(r!)^{-1}x^r and Y_r=bar(r!)^{-1}y^r. For all i,j∈I the K-linear coordinate matrix unit T_ij relative to β is bar(binom(λ,j))^{-1}X_{λ-i}Y_λ X_λ Y_{λ-j}. Consequently every K-linear subspace of V(K) invariant under x,y is invariant under every T_ij.\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D002\",\"D003\",\"D004\",\"D006\",\"D007\",\"D008\",\"D009\",\"D012\",\"D013\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"ρ\",\"type\":\"ring map R→K\"},{\"symbol\":\"a\",\"type\":\"integer\"},{\"symbol\":\"d\",\"type\":\"integer not divisible by p\"},{\"symbol\":\"b\",\"type\":\"nonzero element of J with J=Rb\"},{\"symbol\":\"β\",\"type\":\"K-basis of V(K) indexed by I\"},{\"symbol\":\"i\",\"type\":\"element of I\"},{\"symbol\":\"j\",\"type\":\"element of I\"},{\"symbol\":\"r\",\"type\":\"nonnegative integer\"},{\"symbol\":\"T\",\"type\":\"family of coordinate matrix units on V(K) indexed by I×I\"}],\"objects\":[\"ρ\",\"R\",\"K\",\"a\",\"d\",\"p\",\"V(K)\",\"M\",\"M_R\",\"b\",\"J\",\"e\",\"β\",\"I\",\"n\",\"λ\",\"x\",\"y\",\"h\",\"X\",\"Y\",\"i\",\"j\",\"r\",\"T\"],\"origin\":\"problem.md:150-170; both tensor identifications and their maps are retained.\"},{\"id\":\"P\",\"kind\":\"target\",\"definitions\":[\"D013\",\"D014\"],\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"W\",\"type\":\"K-linear subspace of V(K)\"}],\"objects\":[\"V(K)\",\"L(K)\",\"K\",\"W\",\"x\",\"y\",\"h\",\"λ\",\"p\"]}],\"inferences\":[{\"id\":\"R001\",\"inputs\":[\"H003\",\"H004\",\"H005\",\"M003\"],\"output\":\"N001\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D002\",\"D003\",\"D004\",\"D006\"],\"rules\":[\"direct\",\"natural_induction\",\"forall_introduction\"],\"locals\":[{\"symbol\":\"m\",\"type\":\"nonnegative integer\",\"scope\":\"commutator induction\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S001\",\"kind\":\"arbitrary\",\"declaration\":\"Use m≥0 as the induction index for the formulas for h y^m v and x y^m v.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"y\",\"v\"]}],\"argument\":[{\"id\":\"S001\",\"text\":\"H003 gives n>0 and I has n elements. We prove the two string formulas by induction. At m=0, h v=λv and xv=0 by H005. For the step temporarily assume L1: h y^m v=(λ-2m)y^m v, and, if m≥1, x y^m v=m(λ-m+1)y^{m-1}v (with xy^0v=0 at m=0). From [h,y]=-2y and [x,y]=h in H004, h y^{m+1}v=y h y^m v-2y^{m+1}v=(λ-2m-2)y^{m+1}v, while x y^{m+1}v=y x y^m v+h y^m v=[m(λ-m+1)+λ-2m]y^m v=(m+1)(λ-m)y^m v. This also holds at m=0 using xv=0. Discharge L1; induction proves the asserted formulas for all nonnegative m.\",\"uses\":[\"H003\",\"H004\",\"H005\"],\"uses_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"I\",\"λ\",\"h\",\"x\",\"y\",\"v\",\"m\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F001\",\"statement\":\"n>0, card(I)=n; for every m≥0, h y^m v=(λ-2m)y^m v, and for every m≥1, x y^m v=m(λ-m+1)y^{m-1}v.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"m\",\"type\":\"nonnegative integer\"}],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"I\",\"λ\",\"h\",\"x\",\"y\",\"v\",\"m\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[\"L1\"],\"discharges\":[\"L1\"]},{\"id\":\"S002\",\"text\":\"For every i∈I, iteration of F001 gives x^i y^i v=i! λ(λ-1)⋯(λ-i+1)v. For i=0 this is v with empty product 1. For 1≤i≤λ each factor is a positive integer and hence nonzero in ℂ. H005 gives v≠0, so y^iv and e_i are nonzero. Their h-eigenvalues λ-2i are pairwise distinct in ℂ. To check independence, take any relation ∑_{i∈I}c_i e_i=0 with complex coefficients and any j∈I; apply ∏_{k∈I,k≠j}(h-(λ-2k)Id). Every term except the j-th is zero; the remaining term is c_j∏_{k≠j}2(k-j)e_j=0. The product and e_j are nonzero, so c_j=0. Thus the whole family is linearly independent.\",\"uses\":[\"H005\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\"],\"objects\":[\"I\",\"λ\",\"e\",\"x\",\"y\",\"h\",\"v\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F002\",\"statement\":\"The family e:I→V is linearly independent over ℂ, each e_i is nonzero, and h e_i=(λ-2i)e_i for every i∈I.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"i\",\"type\":\"element of I\"}],\"objects\":[\"e\",\"I\",\"V\",\"h\",\"λ\",\"i\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S003\",\"text\":\"Apply M003 with A=ℂ, T=V, Q=I and f=e. F001 makes I finite and nonempty; F002 supplies independence; H004 and card(I)=n=λ+1 give the required dimension equality. Thus e spans V, so together with independence it is a basis. F001 at m=λ+1 says y^{λ+1}v, if nonzero, is an eigenvector of eigenvalue -λ-2. Expand this vector in the e-basis. Applying h-(-λ-2)Id shows each coefficient times (λ-2i+λ+2)=2(λ+1-i) is zero; these coefficients are nonzero scalars for i∈I. Hence every coordinate is zero and y^{λ+1}v=0. All higher powers vanish by further application of y.\",\"uses\":[\"H004\",\"M003\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\",\"F002\"],\"objects\":[\"V\",\"I\",\"e\",\"n\",\"λ\",\"y\",\"v\",\"h\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F003\",\"statement\":\"The family e is a complex basis of V, and y^m v=0 for all integers m>λ.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"m\",\"type\":\"integer greater than λ\"}],\"objects\":[\"e\",\"V\",\"y\",\"v\",\"m\",\"λ\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S004\",\"text\":\"Divide the string identities F001 by i!: e_0=v, xe_0=0, xe_i=(λ-i+1)e_{i-1} for 1≤i≤λ, he_i=(λ-2i)e_i; directly ye_i=(i+1)e_{i+1} for i<λ, and ye_λ=0 by F003. For r≤i, iteration and division by r! give X_r e_i=[(λ-i+1)⋯(λ-i+r)/r!]e_{i-r}=binom(λ-i+r,r)e_{i-r}; if r>i the iteration passes xe_0=0. Similarly Y_r e_i=[(i+1)⋯(i+r)/r!]e_{i+r}=binom(i+r,r)e_{i+r} when i+r≤λ, and otherwise is zero by F003. These binomial numbers are integers (they count r-element subsets of a set of the indicated nonnegative size). These are all the clauses of N001.\",\"uses\":[\"H005\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\",\"F002\",\"F003\"],\"objects\":[\"e\",\"I\",\"λ\",\"x\",\"y\",\"h\",\"v\",\"X\",\"Y\"],\"produces\":[],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]}],\"applications\":[{\"input\":\"M003\",\"at\":\"S003\",\"bindings\":\"A=ℂ, T=V, Q=I, f=e.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"ℂ is a field and V is the complex representation space in the parameters.\",\"F001 gives card(I)=n>0.\",\"F002 proves linear independence by the isolating polynomial.\",\"H004 gives dim_ℂ V=λ+1=n=card(I) by F001.\"]}]},{\"id\":\"R002\",\"inputs\":[\"H001\",\"H003\",\"H004\",\"H006\",\"N001\",\"M001\",\"M002\",\"M006\"],\"output\":\"N002\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D002\",\"D003\",\"D004\",\"D005\",\"D006\",\"D007\",\"D008\",\"D009\",\"D010\"],\"rules\":[\"direct\",\"exists_elimination\",\"forall_introduction\"],\"locals\":[{\"symbol\":\"f\",\"type\":\"integer basis of M indexed by I, also a complex basis of V\",\"scope\":\"lattice localization\",\"start\":\"S002\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"witness\",\"declaration\":\"Choose an integer basis f=(f_i)_{i∈I} of M that is a complex basis of V, using F001.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[\"F001\"],\"objects\":[\"I\",\"M\",\"V\"]}],\"argument\":[{\"id\":\"S001\",\"text\":\"By H006 choose an integer basis of M that is also a complex basis of V. Its index set is finite because V has finite complex dimension (H004). M002 applied to this complex basis over ℂ counts its elements as dim_ℂ V=n. N001 gives card(I)=n, so reindexing gives an integer basis indexed by I. This proves F001. H001 ensures R is a subring: products of integers not divisible by the prime p are again not divisible by p, and addition and multiplication of the stated fractions preserve the denominator condition.\",\"uses\":[\"H001\",\"H004\",\"H006\",\"N001\",\"M002\"],\"uses_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"M\",\"V\",\"n\",\"I\",\"R\",\"p\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F001\",\"statement\":\"There exists an integer basis of M indexed by I that is also a complex basis of V.\",\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[\"M\",\"I\",\"V\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S002\",\"text\":\"Choose f as in F001. M001 with A=ℤ, B=R and T=M gives the R-basis (1⊗f_i)_{i∈I} of M_R. Under ι_R these vectors go to f_i. If ∑ r_i f_i=0 with r_i∈R, complex independence of f gives every r_i=0. Hence ι_R is injective. Its image contains f and therefore spans V over ℂ. This also proves that M_R is free of rank n. From now on identify M_R with this image.\",\"uses\":[\"M001\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\"],\"objects\":[\"f\",\"I\",\"R\",\"M\",\"M_R\",\"ι_R\",\"V\",\"n\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F002\",\"statement\":\"ι_R is injective; M_R has R-basis (1⊗f_i)_{i∈I}, has rank n, and its image spans V over ℂ.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"i\",\"type\":\"element of I\"}],\"objects\":[\"ι_R\",\"M_R\",\"f\",\"I\",\"n\",\"V\",\"i\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S003\",\"text\":\"Admissibility H006 makes every X_r,Y_r preserve M; their complex linearity then preserves the R-span M_R. In particular x=X_1 and y=Y_1 preserve M, and h=[x,y]=xy-yx does too by H004. These integral operators obey the Chevalley brackets on M and therefore induce the stated action after tensoring. For any 0≤t≤λ, H003 gives t<p; applying M006 with ℓ=p to this arbitrary t and H001 gives p coprime to t!, hence p does not divide t!. Thus 1/t!∈R and t! is a unit. In particular λ!, j!, (λ-j)! are units for j∈I. The integer identity binom(λ,j)j!(λ-j)!=λ! shows that binom(λ,j) is a unit of R, with inverse j!(λ-j)!/λ!.\",\"uses\":[\"H001\",\"H003\",\"H004\",\"H006\",\"M006\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F002\"],\"objects\":[\"M\",\"M_R\",\"R\",\"X\",\"Y\",\"x\",\"y\",\"h\",\"p\",\"λ\",\"I\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F003\",\"statement\":\"All X_r,Y_r preserve M_R, and x,y,h preserve M. For 0≤t≤λ, t! is a unit of R; for every j∈I, binom(λ,j) is a unit of R.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"r\",\"type\":\"nonnegative integer\"},{\"symbol\":\"t\",\"type\":\"integer with 0≤t≤λ\"},{\"symbol\":\"j\",\"type\":\"element of I\"}],\"objects\":[\"X\",\"Y\",\"M_R\",\"M\",\"x\",\"y\",\"h\",\"t\",\"λ\",\"R\",\"j\",\"I\",\"r\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S004\",\"text\":\"Compute on the complex e-basis of N001. X_λ kills all e_k with k<λ and sends e_λ to e_0 with coefficient binom(λ,λ)=1; Y_λ sends e_0 to e_λ with coefficient 1. Thus Y_λ X_λ=E_{λλ}. Also X_{λ-i}e_λ=e_i with coefficient 1, so X_{λ-i}E_{λλ}=E_{iλ}. For E_{λλ}Y_{λ-j}, an input e_k with k<j is sent below e_λ and killed by the projection; if k>j it is sent beyond the end of the string and is zero. On e_j the coefficient is binom(λ,λ-j)=binom(λ,j). Thus E_{λλ}Y_{λ-j}=binom(λ,j)E_{λj}. Composing gives E_ij=binom(λ,j)^{-1}X_{λ-i}Y_λ X_λ Y_{λ-j}. Every factor preserves M_R by F003, and its scalar lies in R. Hence all E_ij preserve M_R. This completes N002 without assuming any distinction of weights modulo p.\",\"uses\":[\"N001\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F002\",\"F003\"],\"objects\":[\"I\",\"e\",\"λ\",\"X\",\"Y\",\"E\",\"M_R\",\"R\",\"p\"],\"produces\":[],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]}],\"applications\":[{\"input\":\"M002\",\"at\":\"S001\",\"bindings\":\"A=ℂ, T=V, f the complex basis supplied by H006, Q its finite index set.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"ℂ is a field, hence satisfies the strong rank condition.\",\"V is a complex vector space.\",\"H006 supplies a complex basis; its index set is finite since H004 gives finite dimension.\"]},{\"input\":\"M001\",\"at\":\"S002\",\"bindings\":\"A=ℤ, B=R, T=M, Q=I, basis f from F001, for every i∈I.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"ℤ is commutative and R is the rational subring established in S001, with its integer algebra map.\",\"M is an abelian group and ℤ-module.\",\"The chosen f is an integer basis by F001; its indices are exactly I.\"]},{\"input\":\"M006\",\"at\":\"S003\",\"bindings\":\"ℓ=p and t an arbitrary integer 0≤t≤λ regarded as a natural number.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"H001 gives primality of p.\",\"H003 and t≤λ give t<p.\"]}]},{\"id\":\"R003\",\"inputs\":[\"H001\",\"N001\",\"N002\",\"M001\",\"M002\",\"M003\",\"M004\",\"M005\",\"M007\"],\"output\":\"N003\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D002\",\"D006\",\"D007\",\"D008\",\"D009\",\"D010\",\"D011\",\"D012\"],\"rules\":[\"direct\",\"exists_elimination\",\"forall_introduction\"],\"locals\":[{\"symbol\":\"T\",\"type\":\"ℚ-vector space\",\"scope\":\"rationalization\",\"start\":\"S002\",\"end\":\"S005\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Put T=ℚ⊗_R J with the usual ℚ-vector space structure.\",\"from_inputs\":[\"H001\"],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"R\",\"J\"]},{\"symbol\":\"r\",\"type\":\"natural number\",\"scope\":\"rank calculation\",\"start\":\"S004\",\"end\":\"S005\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Put r=dim_ℚ T.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[\"F002\"],\"objects\":[\"T\"]}],\"argument\":[{\"id\":\"S001\",\"text\":\"Use the embedding of N002. E_ii maps M_R onto Jcoord_i e_i, because it extracts the i-th coordinate on the N001 basis. For i,j∈I and c∈Jcoord_j, E_ij(c e_j)=c e_i belongs to M_R by N002, so c∈Jcoord_i. Interchanging i,j proves equality. N001 gives 0∈I and e_0=v, hence the common module is J. The projection E_00 of a finite R-basis of M_R (N002) gives finitely many generators of J e_0; since e_0≠0, their coefficients generate J. If J were zero every projection E_ii would vanish on M_R, and their sum is Id_V, so M_R would be zero. This contradicts its complex span V and n>0 from N001. Finally, coordinate projections show each element of M_R has coordinates in J; conversely each sum ∑ c_i e_i with c_i∈J belongs to M_R. Uniqueness is complex independence of e. Thus M_R=⊕_{i∈I}J e_i.\",\"uses\":[\"N001\",\"N002\"],\"uses_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"E\",\"I\",\"Jcoord\",\"J\",\"e\",\"M_R\",\"R\",\"V\",\"v\",\"n\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F001\",\"statement\":\"n>0; Jcoord_i=J for all i∈I; J is nonzero and finitely generated over R; M_R consists exactly of the sums ∑_{i∈I}c_i e_i with c_i∈J and these coordinates are unique.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"i\",\"type\":\"element of I\"}],\"objects\":[\"n\",\"Jcoord\",\"J\",\"I\",\"R\",\"M_R\",\"e\",\"i\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S002\",\"text\":\"Put T=ℚ⊗_R J. The multiplication map μ:T→ℂ is defined on simple tensors by μ(q⊗c)=qc. It is well-defined by additivity and R-balancing. Every tensor is a finite sum of simple tensors. Write its finitely many rational first factors over a common nonzero integer denominator d; balancing integer numerators into J writes the tensor as (1/d)⊗c for one c∈J. If its image in ℂ is zero, c/d=0, so c=0 and the tensor is zero. Thus μ is injective. If c_1,…,c_s is a finite R-generating family of J from F001, then 1⊗c_1,…,1⊗c_s spans T over ℚ by tensor bilinearity. In particular T is finitely generated as a ℚ-module, that is finite dimensional.\",\"uses\":[\"H001\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\"],\"objects\":[\"T\",\"R\",\"J\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F002\",\"statement\":\"T=ℚ⊗_R J is a finite-dimensional ℚ-vector space, and μ:T→ℂ, μ(q⊗c)=qc, is injective.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"μ\",\"type\":\"ℚ-linear map T→ℂ\"},{\"symbol\":\"q\",\"type\":\"rational number\"},{\"symbol\":\"c\",\"type\":\"element of J\"}],\"objects\":[\"T\",\"R\",\"J\",\"μ\",\"q\",\"c\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S003\",\"text\":\"The coordinate isomorphism in F001 induces an explicit ℚ-linear equivalence Φ:ℚ⊗_R M_R→∏_{i∈I}T: on q⊗∑c_i e_i it gives (q⊗c_i)_{i∈I}. For clarity, define its inverse by summing the maps T→ℚ⊗_R M_R induced by c↦c e_i: a tuple (q_i⊗c_i)_i maps to ∑_i q_i⊗(c_i e_i), extended additively in each entry. Both maps are defined by balanced bilinear maps. On simple tensors their composites are identities, by uniqueness of the coordinates and finite distributivity; simple tensors generate, so these checks prove both inverse identities. This supplies the rationalized direct-sum identification, not just a count.\",\"uses\":[],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\",\"F002\"],\"objects\":[\"T\",\"I\",\"R\",\"M_R\",\"J\",\"e\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F003\",\"statement\":\"There is a ℚ-linear equivalence Φ:ℚ⊗_R M_R≃∏_{i∈I}T sending q⊗∑c_i e_i to (q⊗c_i)_{i∈I}.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"Φ\",\"type\":\"ℚ-linear equivalence ℚ⊗_R M_R≃∏_{i∈I}T\"},{\"symbol\":\"q\",\"type\":\"rational number\"},{\"symbol\":\"c\",\"type\":\"family I→J\"}],\"objects\":[\"Φ\",\"R\",\"M_R\",\"I\",\"T\",\"e\",\"q\",\"c\",\"J\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S004\",\"text\":\"Put r=dim_ℚ T. N002 supplies an R-basis of M_R indexed by I. Applying M001 with A=R and B=ℚ to this basis gives a ℚ-basis of ℚ⊗_R M_R indexed by I; M002 over ℚ therefore gives its dimension as card(I)=n, using N001. Apply M007 with A=ℚ to T to obtain freeness. The finite generation in F002 and this freeness verify the module premises of M004. Thus M004 with the constant family T over I gives dim_ℚ(∏_{i∈I}T)=∑_{i∈I}r=nr. M005 applied to Φ from F003 equates these dimensions: n=nr. Because n>0, r=1.\",\"uses\":[\"N001\",\"N002\",\"M001\",\"M002\",\"M004\",\"M005\",\"M007\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\",\"F002\",\"F003\"],\"objects\":[\"r\",\"T\",\"R\",\"M_R\",\"I\",\"n\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F004\",\"statement\":\"dim_ℚ T=r=1.\",\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[\"T\",\"r\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S005\",\"text\":\"For any nonzero a∈J, injectivity of μ in F002 gives 1⊗a≠0. The singleton family consisting of 1⊗a is linearly independent over ℚ: a scalar annihilating a nonzero vector in a vector space over a field is zero. Apply M003 with A=ℚ, ambient space T, the singleton index type and this singleton family. F004 gives the required dimension 1, so every 1⊗c with c∈J is q(1⊗a) for some q∈ℚ. Applying μ gives c=qa. Together with F001–F004 this proves every assertion of N003.\",\"uses\":[\"M003\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\",\"F002\",\"F004\"],\"objects\":[\"T\",\"J\",\"r\"],\"produces\":[],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]}],\"applications\":[{\"input\":\"M001\",\"at\":\"S004\",\"bindings\":\"A=R, B=ℚ, source module=M_R, index set I, using any R-basis provided by N002.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"R is the commutative rational subring defined under H001 and ℚ is its fraction-field algebra.\",\"M_R is an R-module by D008.\",\"N002 supplies the R-basis indexed by I; the formula is used for all its indices.\"]},{\"input\":\"M002\",\"at\":\"S004\",\"bindings\":\"A=ℚ, source module=ℚ⊗_R M_R, basis obtained in S004 by M001, Q=I.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"ℚ is a field and hence has the strong rank condition.\",\"The tensor scalar extension is a ℚ-vector space.\",\"M001 gives the basis and N001 gives the finite cardinality n of I.\"]},{\"input\":\"M007\",\"at\":\"S004\",\"bindings\":\"A=ℚ, source module=T.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"ℚ is a field.\",\"F002 constructs T as a ℚ-vector space, in particular an additive commutative group and module.\"]},{\"input\":\"M004\",\"at\":\"S004\",\"bindings\":\"A=ℚ, Q=I, constant family with each fiber T.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"ℚ is a field and hence satisfies the strong rank condition.\",\"N001 gives finite I; each fiber is the vector space T from F002.\",\"M007 applied earlier in this paragraph gives freeness of T over ℚ.\",\"F002 displays a finite spanning set of T over ℚ.\"]},{\"input\":\"M005\",\"at\":\"S004\",\"bindings\":\"A=ℚ, source=ℚ⊗_R M_R, target=∏_{i∈I}T, f=Φ from F003.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"Both objects are ℚ-vector spaces, as constructed in F002–F003.\",\"F003 gives the explicit balanced maps and checks their two inverse identities.\"]},{\"input\":\"M003\",\"at\":\"S005\",\"bindings\":\"A=ℚ, source space=T, Q a singleton type, family its sole value 1⊗a, for arbitrary a∈J with a≠0.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"ℚ is a field and F002 gives the ℚ-vector space T.\",\"The singleton index type is finite and nonempty.\",\"Injectivity in F002 and a≠0 show 1⊗a≠0; field scalar cancellation proves singleton independence.\",\"F004 gives dim_ℚ T=1, the singleton cardinality.\"]}]},{\"id\":\"R004\",\"inputs\":[\"H001\",\"N003\"],\"output\":\"N004\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D002\",\"D006\",\"D007\",\"D008\",\"D009\",\"D012\"],\"rules\":[\"direct\",\"exists_elimination\"],\"locals\":[{\"symbol\":\"a\",\"type\":\"nonzero element of J\",\"scope\":\"valuation-minimum construction\",\"start\":\"S002\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"witness\",\"declaration\":\"Choose a∈J with a≠0 using the nonzero module assertion of N003.\",\"from_inputs\":[\"N003\"],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"J\"]},{\"symbol\":\"g\",\"type\":\"nonempty finite family of nonzero elements generating J over R\",\"scope\":\"valuation-minimum construction\",\"start\":\"S002\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"witness\",\"declaration\":\"Choose a nonempty finite generating family g for J whose entries are nonzero, using F001.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[\"F001\"],\"objects\":[\"J\",\"R\"]}],\"argument\":[{\"id\":\"S001\",\"text\":\"N003 says J is nonzero and finitely generated. Remove all zero members of a finite generating family. The remaining family still generates, and cannot be empty because J≠0. This proves F001, which will allow the finite minimum construction.\",\"uses\":[\"N003\"],\"uses_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"J\",\"R\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F001\",\"statement\":\"There exists a nonempty finite family of nonzero elements generating J as an R-module.\",\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[\"J\",\"R\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S002\",\"text\":\"Choose a≠0 in J and a family g as in F001. By the rational-line conclusion of N003, every g_j is q_j a for some q_j∈ℚ; because g_j and a are nonzero, q_j≠0. For a nonzero rational q=A/B with nonzero integers A,B, repeatedly remove factors p from A and B. This process terminates since p≥2 by H001 and absolute values strictly decrease at each removal. Thus A=p^s A', B=p^t B' with s,t≥0 and p dividing neither A' nor B'. Consequently q=p^{s-t}u with u=A'/B' a unit of R: both u and u^{-1}=B'/A' satisfy the denominator condition. Apply this to all the finitely many q_j, writing q_j=p^{ν_j}u_j with ν_j∈ℤ and u_j∈R×. The nonempty finite list of integer exponents has a minimum ν and an index j_0 attaining it.\",\"uses\":[\"H001\",\"N003\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\"],\"objects\":[\"a\",\"g\",\"J\",\"R\",\"p\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F002\",\"statement\":\"For the chosen nonempty generating family g there are nonzero rationals q_j and units u_j∈R with g_j=q_j a=p^{ν_j}u_j a, integer exponents ν_j, and a minimum exponent ν attained at some index j_0.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"q\",\"type\":\"family of nonzero rationals indexed by g\"},{\"symbol\":\"u\",\"type\":\"family of units of R indexed by g\"},{\"symbol\":\"ν\",\"type\":\"minimum integer exponent\"},{\"symbol\":\"j_0\",\"type\":\"index of g attaining the minimum\"}],\"objects\":[\"g\",\"a\",\"R\",\"p\",\"q\",\"u\",\"ν\",\"j_0\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S003\",\"text\":\"Using the notation quantified in F002, put b=p^ν a=u_{j_0}^{-1}g_{j_0}. This is a nonzero element of J, since p^ν is a nonzero rational and a≠0. Every generator g_j equals p^{ν_j-ν}u_j b with ν_j-ν≥0, so g_j∈Rb; conversely b∈J gives Rb⊂J. Therefore J=Rb. Substituting into the unique-coordinate decomposition of N003 proves that every element of M_R is a unique R-linear combination of b e_i: if ∑r_i b e_i=0, N003 gives r_i b=0 for each i, hence r_i=0 since b≠0. Thus these vectors are an R-basis indexed by I, of cardinality n>0. The same argument applies to any nonzero b∈J with J=Rb, proving N004.\",\"uses\":[\"N003\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F002\"],\"objects\":[\"a\",\"g\",\"J\",\"p\",\"R\",\"M_R\",\"e\",\"I\",\"n\"],\"produces\":[],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]}],\"applications\":[]},{\"id\":\"R005\",\"inputs\":[\"H001\",\"H002\",\"N001\",\"N002\",\"N004\",\"M001\",\"M002\"],\"output\":\"N005\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D002\",\"D003\",\"D004\",\"D006\",\"D007\",\"D008\",\"D009\",\"D010\",\"D012\",\"D013\"],\"rules\":[\"direct\",\"exists_elimination\",\"forall_introduction\",\"implication_introduction\"],\"locals\":[{\"symbol\":\"ρ\",\"type\":\"ring homomorphism R→K\",\"scope\":\"reduction\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S005\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Define ρ(a/d)=bar(a)/bar(d), where bars are integer images in K.\",\"from_inputs\":[\"H001\",\"H002\"],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"R\",\"K\",\"p\"]},{\"symbol\":\"b\",\"type\":\"nonzero element of J with J=Rb\",\"scope\":\"basis reduction\",\"start\":\"S003\",\"end\":\"S005\",\"kind\":\"witness\",\"declaration\":\"Choose b∈J, b≠0 and J=Rb using N004.\",\"from_inputs\":[\"N004\"],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"J\",\"R\"]},{\"symbol\":\"β\",\"type\":\"family I→V(K)\",\"scope\":\"basis reduction\",\"start\":\"S003\",\"end\":\"S005\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"declaration\":\"Let β_i be the image of (b e_i)⊗1 under the inverse of the first isomorphism in F001.\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[\"F001\"],\"objects\":[\"b\",\"e\",\"I\",\"V(K)\"]}],\"argument\":[{\"id\":\"S001\",\"text\":\"For every integer d not divisible by p its image in K is nonzero, because H002 says the kernel of ℤ→K is pℤ. Thus define ρ(a/d)=bar(a)/bar(d). Equality of fractions is preserved by cross-multiplication, so this is well-defined and preserves sums and products; it extends ℤ→K. The characteristic also factors the integer map through ℱ_p=ℤ/pℤ; primality H001 makes this a field, and its map into K is injective since a unital map from a field has zero kernel. On elementary tensors define A:M⊗_ℤK→M_R⊗_R K by A(m⊗c)=(1⊗m)⊗c. Its inverse sends (r⊗m)⊗c to m⊗ρ(r)c. The balancing relations in the two tensor products verify well-definedness; the inverse identities follow on the displayed generators, since (r⊗m)⊗c=(1⊗m)⊗ρ(r)c. These generators span, so A is a K-linear isomorphism.\",\"uses\":[\"H001\",\"H002\"],\"uses_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"ρ\",\"R\",\"K\",\"p\",\"M\",\"M_R\",\"V(K)\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F001\",\"statement\":\"ρ:R→K, ρ(a/d)=bar(a)/bar(d), is a ring map extending ℤ→K, and the map A:V(K)→M_R⊗_R K given by m⊗c↦(1⊗m)⊗c is a K-linear isomorphism with inverse (r⊗m)⊗c↦m⊗ρ(r)c.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"A\",\"type\":\"K-linear isomorphism V(K)≃M_R⊗_R K\"},{\"symbol\":\"a\",\"type\":\"integer\"},{\"symbol\":\"d\",\"type\":\"integer not divisible by p\"},{\"symbol\":\"m\",\"type\":\"element of M\"},{\"symbol\":\"c\",\"type\":\"element of K\"},{\"symbol\":\"r\",\"type\":\"element of R\"}],\"objects\":[\"ρ\",\"R\",\"K\",\"p\",\"M\",\"M_R\",\"V(K)\",\"A\",\"a\",\"d\",\"m\",\"c\",\"r\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S002\",\"text\":\"The kernel of ρ is pR: for a/d∈R, its image is zero exactly when p divides a, equivalently a/d belongs to pR. Its image is the prime field ℱ_p, because a/d reduces to the quotient of two prime-field elements. Thus R/pR≃ℱ_p. The map M_R⊗_R K→(M_R/pM_R)⊗_{ℱ_p}K sends u⊗c to [u]⊗c. Conversely define [u]⊗c↦u⊗c. A different lift u+pw gives the same tensor since (pw)⊗c=w⊗pc=0. Balancing over ℱ_p is valid: lift a prime-field scalar to r∈R and use (ru)⊗c=u⊗ρ(r)c; changing its lift by pR has no effect. These formulas define inverse K-linear maps on the simple tensors. This proves the second isomorphism as well as its compatibility with the first one.\",\"uses\":[\"H001\",\"H002\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\"],\"objects\":[\"ρ\",\"R\",\"M_R\",\"K\",\"p\",\"V(K)\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F002\",\"statement\":\"R/pR≃ℱ_p via ρ, and B:M_R⊗_R K≃(M_R/pM_R)⊗_{ℱ_p}K is the K-linear isomorphism u⊗c↦[u]⊗c with inverse [u]⊗c↦u⊗c.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"B\",\"type\":\"K-linear isomorphism M_R⊗_R K≃(M_R/pM_R)⊗_{ℱ_p}K\"},{\"symbol\":\"u\",\"type\":\"element of M_R\"},{\"symbol\":\"c\",\"type\":\"element of K\"}],\"objects\":[\"R\",\"ρ\",\"M_R\",\"p\",\"K\",\"B\",\"u\",\"c\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S003\",\"text\":\"Choose b as in N004 and define β_i=A^{-1}((b e_i)⊗1). M001 with A=R, B=K and basis (b e_i) gives the K-basis 1⊗b e_i of K⊗_R M_R; the tensor flip sends it to (b e_i)⊗1 in M_R⊗_R K. The flip is an isomorphism because u⊗c↦c⊗u and its reverse are balanced and their composites fix all simple tensors. Transporting along A^{-1} therefore shows that β is a K-basis of V(K). M002 over K counts it: dim_K V(K)=card(I)=n>0 by N001. Also the quotient M_R/pM_R has the basis [b e_i] over R/pR: coordinates in the R-basis of N004 reduce independently, since a combination lies in pM_R exactly when every coefficient lies in pR. Thus β is precisely the reduced basis extended from ℱ_p to K as in the supplied proof.\",\"uses\":[\"N001\",\"N004\",\"M001\",\"M002\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\",\"F002\"],\"objects\":[\"b\",\"β\",\"J\",\"R\",\"M_R\",\"K\",\"e\",\"I\",\"n\",\"p\",\"V(K)\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F003\",\"statement\":\"β is a K-basis of V(K) indexed by I, dim_K V(K)=n>0; it is the scalar extension to K of the ℱ_p-basis [b e_i] of M_R/pM_R.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"i\",\"type\":\"element of I\"}],\"objects\":[\"β\",\"V(K)\",\"K\",\"I\",\"n\",\"b\",\"e\",\"M_R\",\"p\",\"i\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S004\",\"text\":\"N001 gives all action formulas on e_i; complex linearity gives the identical formulas on b e_i. N002 says the operators preserve M_R and x,y,h preserve M. The maps A and B above commute with every induced lattice operator: check this on m⊗c and u⊗c using their defining formulas. Therefore the x,y,h formulas on β and the X_r,Y_r formulas are exactly those of N001 with each integer coefficient replaced by its image in K. Explicitly xβ_0=0, xβ_i=bar(λ-i+1)β_{i-1}, yβ_i=bar(i+1)β_{i+1} until yβ_λ=0, and hβ_i=bar(λ-2i)β_i; X_r and Y_r have respectively coefficients bar(binom(λ-i+r,r)) and bar(binom(i+r,r)) in their stated ranges and zero outside. For 0≤r≤λ, N002 makes r! a unit in R, so its image is nonzero in the field K. The integer-operator identities r!X_r=x^r and r!Y_r=y^r hold on M and persist after tensoring. Thus X_r=bar(r!)^{-1}x^r and Y_r=bar(r!)^{-1}y^r.\",\"uses\":[\"N001\",\"N002\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\",\"F002\",\"F003\"],\"objects\":[\"b\",\"β\",\"e\",\"I\",\"λ\",\"X\",\"Y\",\"x\",\"y\",\"h\",\"M\",\"M_R\",\"K\",\"R\",\"V(K)\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F004\",\"statement\":\"The actions on β have all the formulas of N001 with integer coefficients reduced to K. For 0≤r≤λ the scalar bar(r!) is nonzero and X_r=bar(r!)^{-1}x^r, Y_r=bar(r!)^{-1}y^r on V(K).\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"r\",\"type\":\"nonnegative integer\"}],\"objects\":[\"β\",\"X\",\"Y\",\"x\",\"y\",\"h\",\"λ\",\"K\",\"V(K)\",\"r\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S005\",\"text\":\"N002 expresses E_ij=binom(λ,j)^{-1}X_{λ-i}Y_λ X_λ Y_{λ-j} with the scalar a unit in R. Since E_ij(b e_k) is b e_i for k=j and zero otherwise, tensoring this identity yields the coordinate matrix unit T_ij on β with exactly the formula stated in N005. Its scalar is nonzero after reduction, since a unit maps to a unit. For an arbitrary K-subspace W temporarily assume L1: W is invariant under x,y. It is then invariant under their nonnegative powers, their scalar multiples, and their compositions. Every divided power appearing in this formula has index between 0 and λ, so F004 shows that it preserves W. Consequently every T_ij preserves W. Discharge L1 and quantify over W. This proves all parts of N005.\",\"uses\":[\"N002\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\",\"F002\",\"F003\",\"F004\"],\"objects\":[\"β\",\"I\",\"λ\",\"X\",\"Y\",\"E\",\"b\",\"e\",\"K\",\"R\",\"V(K)\",\"x\",\"y\"],\"produces\":[],\"introduces\":[\"L1\"],\"discharges\":[\"L1\"]}],\"applications\":[{\"input\":\"M001\",\"at\":\"S003\",\"bindings\":\"Source A=R, B=K via ρ, source module=M_R, source basis=(b e_i)_{i∈I}.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"R is a commutative ring by H001; F001 constructs the R-algebra structure on the field K.\",\"M_R is an R-module.\",\"N004 supplies the R-basis for the chosen b, and I is its index set.\"]},{\"input\":\"M002\",\"at\":\"S003\",\"bindings\":\"Source A=K, source module=V(K), source basis=β indexed by I, after the displayed flip and A^{-1} transport.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"K is a field and thus satisfies the strong rank condition.\",\"V(K) is a K-vector space by D013.\",\"The preceding application and explicit transport prove that β is a K-basis; N001 gives finite I of cardinality n.\"]}]},{\"id\":\"R006\",\"inputs\":[\"N005\"],\"output\":\"P\",\"definitions\":[\"D001\",\"D002\",\"D013\",\"D014\"],\"rules\":[\"direct\",\"exists_elimination\",\"forall_introduction\",\"implication_introduction\",\"case_elimination\"],\"locals\":[{\"symbol\":\"β\",\"type\":\"K-basis of V(K) indexed by I\",\"scope\":\"irreducibility\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"witness\",\"declaration\":\"Choose a basis β with all the matrix-unit and invariance properties asserted by N005.\",\"from_inputs\":[\"N005\"],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"K\",\"V(K)\",\"I\"]},{\"symbol\":\"W\",\"type\":\"K-linear subspace of V(K)\",\"scope\":\"irreducibility\",\"start\":\"S001\",\"end\":\"S003\",\"kind\":\"arbitrary\",\"declaration\":\"Let W be an arbitrary K-linear subspace of V(K).\",\"from_inputs\":[],\"from_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"K\",\"V(K)\"]}],\"argument\":[{\"id\":\"S001\",\"text\":\"N005 supplies β, a K-basis of V(K), of cardinality n>0. Its member β_0 is nonzero since 0∈I and basis vectors are independent; hence V(K)≠0. Let W be arbitrary and temporarily assume L1: W is invariant under x,y,h. In particular it is invariant under x,y, and N005 therefore says it is invariant under every coordinate matrix unit on β. If W={0}, the required alternative already holds. For the other case temporarily assume L2: W≠{0}. There is a nonzero vector in W. Its expansion in β has a nonzero coordinate, since an expansion with all coordinates zero would be zero.\",\"uses\":[\"N005\"],\"uses_local\":[],\"objects\":[\"β\",\"W\",\"V(K)\",\"K\",\"I\",\"n\",\"x\",\"y\",\"h\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F001\",\"statement\":\"Under L1 and L2 there exist w∈W and coefficients c:I→K with w=∑_{i∈I}c_i β_i≠0 and an index j∈I with c_j≠0; W is invariant under every coordinate matrix unit on β.\",\"binders\":[{\"symbol\":\"w\",\"type\":\"element of W\"},{\"symbol\":\"c\",\"type\":\"family I→K\"},{\"symbol\":\"j\",\"type\":\"element of I\"},{\"symbol\":\"i\",\"type\":\"element of I\"}],\"objects\":[\"w\",\"W\",\"c\",\"I\",\"K\",\"β\",\"j\",\"i\"],\"assumptions\":[\"L1\",\"L2\"]}],\"introduces\":[\"L1\",\"L2\"],\"discharges\":[]},{\"id\":\"S002\",\"text\":\"Choose w,c,j from F001 within the nonzero branch. For every i∈I, applying the coordinate matrix unit T_ij gives T_ij w=c_j β_i∈W, since it kills all other basis coordinates and preserves W. Because c_j≠0 in the field K and W is K-linear, β_i=c_j^{-1}(c_j β_i) belongs to W. Thus every basis vector belongs to W, so its span, all of V(K), is contained in W. The reverse containment is part of W being a subspace of V(K). Hence W=V(K) in the nonzero case. Discharge L2; together with the zero case this proves W={0} or W=V(K) under L1.\",\"uses\":[],\"uses_local\":[\"F001\"],\"objects\":[\"β\",\"W\",\"I\",\"K\",\"V(K)\"],\"produces\":[{\"id\":\"F002\",\"statement\":\"If W is invariant under x,y,h then W={0} or W=V(K).\",\"binders\":[],\"objects\":[\"W\",\"x\",\"y\",\"h\",\"V(K)\"],\"assumptions\":[]}],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[\"L2\",\"L1\"]},{\"id\":\"S003\",\"text\":\"Universal introduction over W proves the subspace assertion from F002, and S001 already proved V(K)≠0. The action is the induced L(K)-action described in N005 and D014; invariance under its generators x,y,h is exactly invariance under their K-linear span, so this is irreducibility as an L(K)-module. No algebraic closure of K was used. At λ=0 the index set has one element and all relevant factorials and binomial coefficients are 1; all displayed arguments remain valid. At p=2 the allowed λ are 0 and 1 by the original range exported through the constructed index and formulas; nothing used distinct reduced h-eigenvalues. These included endpoints satisfy the same conclusion.\",\"uses\":[\"N005\"],\"uses_local\":[\"F002\"],\"objects\":[\"W\",\"β\",\"V(K)\",\"L(K)\",\"K\",\"x\",\"y\",\"h\",\"λ\",\"p\"],\"produces\":[],\"introduces\":[],\"discharges\":[]}],\"applications\":[]}]}}}","status":"completed"}],"error":null,"incomplete_details":null,"tools":[{"type":"function","name":"search_mathlib","description":"在固定 mathlib 的 Lean 源码中搜索正则表达式，返回至多 100 行。","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"query":{"type":"string"}},"required":["query"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"read_mathlib","description":"读取固定 mathlib 中一个 Mathlib/...lean 文件的指定行。","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"path":{"type":"string"},"start_line":{"type":"integer"},"line_count":{"type":"integer"}},"required":["path","start_line","line_count"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"read_task_input","description":"Read one declared frozen document. For JSON use pointer (empty string lists the root); large objects return keys, and strings resume with offset=next_offset. For text use inclusive start/end lines and column=next_column when continuing a long line. Every serialized result is at most 32 KiB. Follow has_more and the returned continuation coordinates; no arbitrary local files are accessible.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string"},"start":{"type":"integer"},"end":{"type":"integer"},"column":{"type":"integer"},"pointer":{"type":"string"},"offset":{"type":"integer"},"limit":{"type":"integer"}},"required":["id"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"read_yui","description":"Read declared Yui rules/templates; no tests, historical runs or personal files.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"path":{"type":"string"},"start":{"type":"integer"},"end":{"type":"integer"},"column":{"type":"integer"}},"required":["path","start","end"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"check_artifact","description":"Check a JSON artifact against the explicit stage contract; this is not automatic mathematical certification.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"artifact_json":{"type":"string"}},"required":["artifact_json"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"submit_artifact","description":"Submit a JSON artifact after its stage checks.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"artifact_json":{"type":"string"}},"required":["artifact_json"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"report_obstacle","description":"Report only a real substantive/suspected mathematical GAP, unavailable source or conflicting original input, with all five located fields. Ordinary unfinished NL0, source registration and JSON bookkeeping are not reports: continue an actual lookup or draft edit. TASK_INCOMPLETE is unavailable for NL0 authoring. Difficulty or a long run is not evidence of a mathematical gap.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"status":{"type":"string","enum":["SUBSTANTIVE_GAP","SUSPECTED_GAP","SOURCE_UNAVAILABLE","INPUT_CONFLICT"]},"location":{"type":"string"},"explanation":{"type":"string"},"basis":{"type":"string"},"partial_progress":{"type":"string"}},"required":["status","location","explanation","basis","partial_progress"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"check_draft","description":"Preferred structured-object form of check_artifact: provide artifact as an actual JSON object, not an escaped JSON string. Same complete validation and acceptance scope.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"artifact":{"type":"object","additionalProperties":true}},"required":["artifact"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"submit_draft","description":"Preferred structured-object form of submit_artifact: provide artifact as an actual JSON object, not an escaped JSON string. Same complete validation and acceptance scope.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"artifact":{"type":"object","additionalProperties":true}},"required":["artifact"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"probe_library","description":"Query a proved Mathlib theorem and its exact signature/axioms in the offline sandbox. Returns located evidence for an M source; the mathematical statement and applicability still require review. The optional module locator uses dotted syntax (Mathlib.Topology.Basic), never a slash path; omit it if unknown.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string"},"declaration":{"type":"string"},"module":{"type":"string"}},"required":["id","declaration"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"find_library_sources","description":"Search pinned foundational metadata by an exact declaration name or ordinary mathematical terms. Ranked lexical candidates are not a theorem whitelist or an applicability judgment. Selected sources still need explicit M roots and complete checks.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"query":{"type":"string"}},"required":["query"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"read_draft","description":"Read a JSON pointer in your own same-stage candidate after an actual structural check. No other file/stage is accessible. For an existing string optionally supply both start_line/end_line to request up to 250 numbered lines and its complete text_sha256 for replace_lines. A read end beyond the text returns the remaining existing lines and actual range; the start must exist. Edits never clamp their range.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"candidate":{"type":"integer"},"pointer":{"type":"string"},"start_line":{"type":"integer"},"end_line":{"type":"integer"}},"required":["candidate","pointer"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"read_author_packet","description":"Same-author closing inspection of one structurally valid own conditional inference: exact direct inputs, output, declarations and own proof. No ancestor bodies, extra H, independent reviewer or Audit A feedback.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"candidate":{"type":"integer"},"inference":{"type":"string"}},"required":["candidate","inference"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"record_author_checks","description":"Save actual same-author notes and readiness against